\description{
Computes single-frame MSSR (\code{\link[=mssr]{mssr()}}) for every frame of an image sequence
and reduces the stack of maps pixel by pixel with one temporal statistic, as
in the temporal MSSR (t-MSSR) of Torres-Garcia et al. (2022, Fig. 7a and
Supplementary Note 5.2). The output is a temporal statistic of nonlinear
structure maps: it is not a quantitative intensity, and no gain in
resolution is implied (see \strong{Interpretation}). \code{mssr_stack()} stores the
per-frame maps so that several statistics can be computed without
recomputing MSSR.
}
\details{
\strong{Definitions.} Per output pixel, \verb{x_1, ..., x_M} are the values of the \code{M}
selected frames' MSSR maps, \code{m} is their mean and \code{d_t = x_t - m}. Following
Supplementary Table S2:\tabular{lll}{
   \code{statistic} \tab Value \tab Minimum \code{M} \cr
   \code{"mean"} \tab \code{m} \tab 1 \cr
   \code{"var"} \tab \verb{(1/M) sum(d_t^2)} \tab 2 \cr
   \code{"tpm"} \tab \code{(sum(x_t))^2} \tab 1 \cr
   \code{"cv"} \tab \code{var / m}, and 0 where \code{m = 0} \tab 2 \cr
   \code{"sofi2"} \tab \verb{(1/(M-1)) sum_\{t=1\}^\{M-1\} d_t d_\{t+1\}} \tab 2 \cr
   \code{"sofi3"} \tab \verb{(1/(M-2)) sum_\{t=1\}^\{M-2\} d_t d_\{t+1\} d_\{t+2\}} \tab 3 \cr
   \code{"sofi4"} \tab \verb{A(0,1,2,3) - A(0,1)A(2,3) - A(0,2)A(1,3) - A(0,3)A(1,2)} \tab 4 \cr
}


For \code{"sofi4"}, \code{A(L)} is the average over \verb{t = 1, ..., M - 3} of the product
of \verb{d_\{t+l\}} for the lags \code{l} in \code{L}; all its terms use the same frames.
\itemize{
\item \code{"var"} is the population variance; the sample variance is larger by
\code{M / (M - 1)}.
\item \code{"tpm"} equals \code{M^2 * mean^2}. For non-negative maps it is a monotone
function of the mean and shows nothing that \code{"mean"} does not; it is offered
because every implementation by the authors computes it.
\item \code{"cv"} is the supplement's "CV", literally \code{Var / Mean}: an index of
dispersion, not the standard deviation divided by the mean. It takes the
sign of the mean, so it is negative where the mean is negative (signed
input). It is 0 where the mean, formed in the scaled range described under
\strong{Computation}, is exactly 0; where the mean is tiny compared with the
variance the ratio becomes very large, and a ratio beyond the numeric range
is refused.
\item \code{"sofi2"} to \code{"sofi4"} are lagged auto-cumulants of signed deviations.
Noise that is independent from frame to frame does not enter their
expectation. Orders 3 and 4 change sign with the emitters' duty cycle, so
displays usually need \code{sign = "absolute"}.
}

The "SOFI" outputs of the authors' Python, MATLAB and Fiji tools are other
statistics, so they do not match these values by design. The Python and
MATLAB tools use absolute deviations at zero lag, which makes their order 2
the variance without the first frame. The Fiji plugin uses absolute
deviations with lags that wrap around the ends of the acquisition. Their
variance divides by \code{M - 1} except in the Python tool. See
\code{vignette("method-concordance")}.

\strong{Interpretation.} \code{"var"}, \code{"cv"} and the SOFI statistics depend on how
the emitters blink and on the noise, and every map is a nonlinear function
of its frame. Treat the outputs as visual aids:
\itemize{
\item Variance, \code{"cv"} and SOFI of per-frame MSSR maps, and higher MSSR orders,
can show two maxima where there is a single emitter.
\item When emitters are rarely on and dim, MSSR of nearly empty frames adds
maxima to every statistic.
\item With \code{amp > 1}, signal-free regions show a sub-pixel grid with the period
\code{amp}; \code{diagnostics$mesh_index} reports its size there (see \strong{Value}).
\item The article recommends Fourier interpolation for temporal analysis. The
package amplifies with the supplement's cubic kernel and mesh
compensation instead; Fourier interpolation is not implemented.
}

No drift, bleaching or background correction is applied. \code{"var"}, \code{"cv"}
and the SOFI statistics treat slow intensity trends (bleaching, drift, focus
changes) as fluctuation, so a large trend inflates them. Correct the frames
first, and record what was done; \code{diagnostics$trend} reports the ratio of the
raw intensity at the end and at the start of the selected frames.

\strong{Computation.} Every statistic is formed from centered deviations in
frame order, in a power-of-two scaled range so that products of up to four
deviations stay representable. The scaling is undone at the end; a value
that would exceed the numeric range, or a nonzero value that would fall
below the smallest representable magnitude, raises
\code{mssrscopR_error_numerical}, and very small results keep only subnormal
precision.
\code{"mean"} and \code{"tpm"} need one pass over the frames. The others need the mean
first and then a second pass. The per-frame maps are stored for it when they
fit (\code{diagnostics$path} \code{"stored"}), else every frame's MSSR is computed again
(\code{"recompute"}), which doubles the time. Both paths give the same values. With
\code{max_memory_bytes = NULL} the maps are stored when they take at most 2 GiB,
counted with the estimate's 100 percent margin for stored maps; with a
budget, they are stored when the estimate fits it, and a call whose
recompute path does not fit is refused. \code{\link[=mssr_estimate_resources]{mssr_estimate_resources()}} reports
both estimates; they are checked before any frame is read. The input array
is read one frame at a time and never copied as a whole. \code{mssr_stack()}
stores every map: without \code{max_memory_bytes} it refuses (with
\code{mssrscopR_error_resource}) maps that would take more than 2 GiB with that
margin.

Each frame must satisfy the checks of \code{\link[=mssr]{mssr()}}; errors name the frame.
Warnings of the frames' \code{\link[=mssr]{mssr()}} calls (negative values, skipped refinement
steps) are collected and given once per class, with the number of frames
concerned. A call-level \code{mssrscopR_warning_unqualified_order} is given for
orders above 3.
}
\examples{
# Two blinking emitters, 40 frames, Poisson noise (synthetic).
set.seed(1)
g <- expand.grid(r = 1:16, c = 1:16)
spot <- function(r0, c0) exp(-((g$r - r0)^2 + (g$c - c0)^2) / (2 * (3 / 2.3548)^2))
x <- array(0, c(16, 16, 40))
for (t in 1:40) {
  on <- stats::runif(2) < 0.4
  x[, , t] <- stats::rpois(256, 20 * (on[1] * spot(8, 7) + on[2] * spot(8, 10)) + 2)
}
s <- mssr_stack(x, fwhm = 3)
v <- mssr_temporal(s, "var")
v
m <- mssr_temporal(s, "mean")
all.equal(mssr_temporal(s, "tpm")$values, 40^2 * m$values^2)
}
